# Exact method request and source scope

Problem2303016 / AMR-022-3016 is Hayman–Lingham Problem3.16, credited to P. J. Rippon, printed p.65/PDF p.66 of the2018 source. The equation is numbered(3.7), whereas the catalog retains an unresolved source-label token. The formula itself is unchanged:

    integral_0^1 cap(E intersect closed B(x,r)) / r^(n−1) dr < infinity.

E is compact in R^n, n>=3. The conclusion concerns only points x **in E**. The theorem that this set is polar is already known via Kellogg; the requested contribution is a **direct proof**, with possible sharpness information. Merely citing Kellogg would not answer the methodological request. Update3.16 reports no progress to the editors.

The proposed proof uses Newtonian energy capacity and measure arguments. It does not invoke Kellogg, Dirichlet irregular-point results, or the equivalence of the displayed criterion with potential-theoretic thinness. The name “thin point” is only shorthand for the integral-finiteness set in this packet.

The precise sharpness results proved are: every positive decrease in the denominator exponent makes the condition finite everywhere on every compact set; every integrable multiplicative weight behaves similarly; and the exceptional set can be an uncountable compact polar set. No assertion is made about arbitrary nonintegrable gauge weakenings.

Hedberg–Wolff, *Thin sets in nonlinear potential theory*, Ann. Inst. Fourier33(4)(1983),161–187, Theorem2 and its direct Borel-set proof on pp.173–174, use a small-Wiener-integral compact subset and its capacitary measure. The current argument credits and specializes that strategy to Newtonian energy while proving the needed weak potential bound explicitly. It does not invoke their nonlinear Theorem1, silently identify Bessel and Newtonian capacities, or claim a new general Kellogg theorem.
